f  who's spending more money in your app  the weightlifters or the yoga people and  then maybe you can adjust adjust sort of  your marketing spend accordingly and  that's basically it just by using events  and user properties you're able to  supply firebase analytics with all the  information it needs to give you some  pretty useful information about your app  in the form of some very nice-looking  graphs so in fact let's take a look at  some of these right now so these are  some of the basic dashboards you'll get  essentially without any other work as  soon as you integrate the firebase SDK  excuse me okay so here we've got active  users these are users who are  interacting with your app over a defined  time period in our case the last 30 days  we have average revenue per user known  as ARPU and average revenue per paying  user known as our pupu which is my  favorite acronym ever we have retention  cohorts which shows you how frequently  people come back to your app over time  and this is usually broken up into sort  of weekly groups or cohorts we have user  engagement how much time people spend  within their app when they're actually  using it your top and that purchases and  if we scroll down a little bit we can  see like who your users are where  they're coming from  and a whole bunch of other useful  information but actually don't want to  spend a lot of time on these initial  dashboards because I'd like to spend a  little more time talking about events so  any event that your app record whether  it's a firebase automatic event or a  custom event that you're logging shows  up here on our events panel and if we  were to click through on one of these we  could start to see more details about  the this event as it occurs within our  app so let's let's take a look at three  of these examples this first report here  is for the in-app purchase event and you  can see we sort of have  again serve through a series of graphs  the number of times this event occurred  again in the last 30 days we can see the  number of unique users or I suppose more  accurately app instances that have  performed this event we can see average  events per user and then the total value  of purchases during this time period we  also have a few custom reports for  foreign app purchases in this case we  can see what the top products are that  our users have purchased what our  average purchase price per item was and  then if we scroll down a bit you can see  again we get some nice demographic  information about the users who are  performing these events as well as an  events per session graph which sometimes  comes in useful and then you can see we  have similar reports for like our level  up event this is one of those suggested  events that firebase analytics already  has predefined for you know game  developers and also an unlock  achievement event on this is a custom  event that the developers of our sample  game bingo blasts have created on their  own and so we can learn a fair amount  just by looking at these stock reports  right like my in-app purchase report  will let me know sort of what products  are most popular and I can do things  like filter this report by some of our  user properties for instance I can  filter our in-app purchase event by  operating system version and that can  sort of start to inform some important  decisions like gee how  backwards-compatible do we want to be  right like if it turns out users who are  on OS 8 are still spending money within  our game well then maybe that makes  sense to kind of make sure that we can  still support those users and we're able  to give you these kind of reports for  for no cost no matter how many millions  upon millions of users your app might  have so maybe this sounds too good to be  true and you're thinking what the catch  is and I'm glad you're silently thinking  that to yourself because that's the rest  of this presentation so I want you take  a look at this graph again or this  report you can see that our in-app  purchase event gives you some very nice  information about some of the custom  event parameters that it gets in right  in our case the product that was  purchased and how much those items cost  you can also see for a predefined event  like our level up event we get a nice  little bar chart that shows you what new  level our users have reached when they  when they reach this event but then if  we go back to our unlock achievement  event here and this is a custom event we  don't see any information  about the actual achievement that our  user has unlocked like right now if we  want to know what achievements are most  popular or which ones are sort of harder  to unlock them we've anticipated or get  any information about these parameters  that are sent with this customer report  we currently can't get any of that  information with this report and  basically here's the deal the way  firebase analytics works is that every  day the service gets millions upon  millions of events for all of your apps  and every few hours they process this  raw data into these generated reports  for you to view at your leisure and  while this and while these reports are  kept around for you to view whenever you  want this raw data is not which means  that if you want to run complex ad-hoc  queries or you know on historical data  or export your data to use with other  analytics packages or you know simply  run custom reports that you don't get  out of the box with firebase analytics  you're gonna need access to that raw  data and that's where bigquery comes in  I'm guessing here at a Google Cloud  event I don't need to give you a whole  lot of detail on what bigquery is but  for those of you watching at home it's  Google's data warehouse in the cloud  that's designed to run very fast queries  over very large amounts of data data  like the kind of analytics event data  and app with a whole bunch of users  might generate and so the idea is that  when you link your firebase account with  bigquery which you can do through this  handy little panel in the firebase  console it's under settings it's a  little gear icon you can look for it  then when all this analytics data from  your clients get sent to firebase  analytics a copy of that data also gets  sent to bigquery and this happens in  near-real-time bigquery stores this data  in sort of a temporary intraday table  and then at the end of the day copies it  over to a more permanent table that you  can keep for as long as you'd like and  so you can query these intraday tables  if you want something resembling  real-time analytics or it can sort of  look at these historical tables if you  want to look at historical data over  time now I should note that if you want  to use bigquery it's not entirely free  you will need to upgrade your firebase  project to the blaze pay-as-you-go plan  and on the bigquery side of things you  will need to pay for the data you store  the cost of the real-time streaming  inserts and the usage however if we were  to take a look at a sample game like  bingo blast which right now has about  20,000 monthly active users across ios  and android record 70 different event  types and 10 different user properties  right now our data storage charges come  up to about 60 cents a month to store  the previous 10 months worth of data our  we're also paying about 12 cents a month  to insert that data into bigquery in  real time and then if we were to run a  simple query across say a month's worth  of android data that cost about one  tenth of a penny if we were to run 50  queries like that everyday across both  Android and iOS we'd be looking at about  $3 a month however it turns out that  bigquery offers you one terabyte of  usage data for free every month and this  falls well under that free tier so this  part actually costs nothing for a grand  total of 72 cents now obviously huge  disclaimer here these figures can vary  considerably depending on how much data  you're collecting how long you keep it a  lap around for and how often and how  complex you're making these queries but  at least for our sample app linking to  bigquery did not involve a huge  financial hit and I should also notice a  note that there are sort of some tools  in place to set quotas or alert you if  pricing goes over what you're expecting  so looking back at our sample app there  are a few questions that I want to be  able to answer that I can't right now  from firebase analytics first give me  some more information about what  achievements were actually unlocked by  this unlock achievement method event  second while it's nice that I can filter  my events by a single user property I'm  actually interested in filtering by two  user properties right like for example  what if I want to see our spend virtual  currency event for our users who speak  English and also have power-ups right  you know filtering by two user  properties at once is not something I  can do right now with the with the  out-of-the-box reports third I want to  generate a few custom reports that span  both my Android a my iOS data and you  know in theory maybe I want to combine  this data with data coming in from  another analytics tool that my app is  using and then fourth I want to see  what's causing my users to spend virtual  currency in my game you know sure I can  see reports on this spend virtual  currency event itself but what are the  events that lead up to that event that's  something that would be pretty valuable  data to me as as someone who wants my  users to spend money so these are all  areas  we're analyzing the data in bigquery  might help and now before we do that for  again to the demos I do want to explain  what our data is gonna look like cuz I  know about you but I sometimes think of  bigquery is like a giant sequel table  right and if you've played with any of  our public sample data like you know the  New York City Taxi rides or hacker news  posts this is kind of what it looks like  and maybe you knew this but I didn't  bigquery is really much more  sophisticated than this like the rows in  bigquery aren't really single rows that  consist of you know one line of data  they're more like individual JSON trees  that are all crammed into each row right  like each of which can contain key value  pairs and arrays and objects and arrays  of objects and that's important  understand when you're looking at  firebase analytics in bigquery because  one of the first things you might assume  is that each row in bigquery corresponds  to a single firebase analytics event and  that's actually not the case  more often a row and bigquery will  consist of a set of user information and  then probably like two or three events  bundled together for that user so let me  let me explain by showing you what our  actual data scheme looks like so our  user info section here it contains a few  Keeffe straightforward key value pairs  like first open time and some kind of  unique ID that you as an app developer  might provide but then they also contain  a number of nested objects that all have  their own set of key value pairs things  like the device that the user is using  their geographic location the traffic  source information what marketing  campaign brought them into your app and  so on we also store user properties that  we've associated with this user in the  firebase Analytics library and these  properties are basically stored as an  array of objects these objects in turn  contain the the name of the key as well  as the value and the value itself is an  object that has an int value string  value double value and so on and you'll  find that in practice most of these are  no except for the property that contains  the actual value you're interested in  this basically allows you to store sort  of any number of user properties in any  format and they're kept inside your  bigquery table in a consistent manner  and the nice thing about sort of keeping  your user properties in a big nested  array like this is that it's there's  basically one less join that you need to  worry about later when you're building  up your queries and you'll start to see  that in the demo and  a little bit so then in addition to all  this user information there's some event  information and as I mentioned earlier  bigquery tends to store these firebase  analytics events as like an array of  three or four event objects all bundled  together in the same row  these also contain some straightforward  key value pairs like the name of the  event and the timestamp when this event  occurred within your app but then they  also contain an array of event  parameters and just like user properties  these parameters have keys and values  and these values themselves are their  own little objects that contain string  value and value double value and so on  and that's pretty much the data  structure that you're going to be  dealing with in firebase analytics in  bigquery so with that in mind let's  revisit my initial list of questions  that I couldn't get answered by firebase  Analytics alone and we'll start with  that first question what achievements  are actually getting unlocked with this  event this is something our game design  team really wants to know so luckily  querying custom parameters like this  within bigquery is something that really  any halfway decent sequel developer  could put together turns out though I am  NOT that developer honestly I barely  know what I'm doing up here luckily I'm  here with Arun who does so he's gonna  walk us through some live examples on  how to perform some of these queries  using some actual firebase to analytics  data in bigquery so uh  Arun might take it away thanks Todd hi  everybody so in order to answer Todd's  questions of which achievements are  getting unlocked in the app let's  actually see some Android code that you  need to implement the Android snippet  here shows the unlock achievement event  being added to firebase with the name of  the achievement coins hundred being  added as a event parameter a custom  string parameter similarly when the user  clicks other achievements in your app  such as user power-ups dobbs and coins  you can add those as achievement names  as well a few slides earlier Todd showed  us the schema of a firebase record and  bigquery the firebase event and the  parameter names that we just logged into  the and in the Android snippet are shown  in a bigquery record as seen here the  unlocked achieve an unlock achievement  event name is stored as a value in the  first events name field and the  corresponding parameter is stored as a  value in the nested parameter string  value field  let's now do a quick demo demo please so  this is bigquery sequel editor and we  are going to use standard sequel for all  our examples today the query results  show us the count of each unlocked  achievement indicating the most popular  achievements in the app this is just the  results that we just ran like a minute  ago as seen here the top achievements in  the app are kinds 500 and Dobb both of  which are equally popular let's now look  at the actual query itself note the uh  nest syntax here in the from class the  uh nest iterates through an array of  firebase objects which are event objects  in this case stored in the event dem  record and produces one draw for each  event that's just a convenience function  for you let's move on to another example  earlier in the presentation Todd showed  you the events report in firebase  analytics console here is how we can  generate the same record in bigquery  demo please  slides please as seen here the results  show the top events and their event  counts the spent virtual currency is  actually a second most popular event in  the app which is great let's look at our  other open questions Todd had asked  about filtering events by multiple user  properties in our example we will filter  the spent virtual currency event by the  device language and powers which are  both user properties demo please  slides please the results shows that  users with device language set to  English and had power-ups in the app  spent 2250 virtual dollars in the app  for a total of two hundred and eighteen  times which is great here is the query  that we just executed the queries in two  parts in the first part of the query we  apply the max function to all of the  rows so that user properties are applied  uniformly to all bundles of events for  the given user and in the second part of  the query we take the results from the  inner query and only select those events  for which both our user property  criteria are satisfied the power-ups and  the language let us now look at other  example open questions let's now see how  to combine our iOS and Android data demo  please this is a cross-device query and  will shortly see it  slides please the results show that  while iOS tablet users are greater than  iOS mobile device users the results are  just the opposite for Android users  which is interesting here is the query  that we just ran it shows how date data  from two different data sources can be  combined in bigquery in this example  notice how we not only join data across  our iOS and Android apps but in addition  we also joined our iOS and Android app  data across multiple dates now turns out  that a sample data is from a purely  mobile app but in some cases you might  also have a web app in which case you're  probably tracking your analytics in a  tool like Google Analytics it turns out  that with bigquery you can combine data  from firebase analytics with other  providers such as the data that you get  from Google Analytics you can join your  iOS Android and your web data in  bigquery to determine user behavior  across all of your apps let's now look  at probably the most important question  in our minds which is on how you might  figure out what's causing users to spend  money in your app in order to answ